>
  <p>
    “Do Google” is not a strategy. Here’s what competent
    <a href="/services/local-seo">local SEO</a> and
    <a href="/services/paid-ads">paid Google</a> look like for home service companies, and what they
    can realistically achieve when the
    <a href="/services/websites">website</a> and ops keep up.
  </p>

  <h2>The pieces that matter</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Google Business Profile</strong>: correct primary category, complete services, real
    address when you can show one, reviews that keep coming, NAP that matches the site.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Website</strong>: keyword-led pages that convert map and ad traffic into calls. If
    organic visitors don’t book, ads won’t save you.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Local Services Ads</strong>: usually the first paid lane. Pay per lead, cheaper proof,
    Google Guaranteed trust. Then Search ads when you need more volume or higher-quality jobs and
    can afford the CPA.
  </p>

  <h2>What “good” looks like in numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Directionally: revenue climbing faster than Google spend (healthy systems often land in a
    several-times return range when tracked at the business level). Calendar filling with
    <em>booked</em> estimates, not a CRM full of tire-kickers an agency calls “leads.” Map-pack
    presence in the neighborhoods you actually want to serve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Case studies on this site (Rewired, Honest Hank’s, Dry Duck, Hooked Up) show different entry
    points, scale what you have, grow the crew, or start from nothing, but the Google spine is the
    same.
  </p>

  <h2>What it can’t do alone</h2>
  <p>
    Google won’t fix a broken sales process, a brand nobody trusts, or an empty truck roster. It
    won’t make Max Conversions invent high-ticket jobs if your account is optimized for the cheapest
    click. And it won’t stay stable forever without reviews and competitive pressure.
  </p>
  <p>
    Good Google marketing is disciplined fundamentals, honest measurement, and patience where organic
    needs time, plus the guts to turn spend down when you’re full and up when you’re ready.
  </p>
